 "literal": "Expanding-waved great ocean surrounded world also / rarely obtained excellence-of gods' land also / both if-weighed equal-weight will-not-be-indeed / flower-like kohl-eyes gold-like body / excellent-lines loins young-woman's / shoulders-exchange occurring days-with to-us-indeed",
 "translation": "Even if we weigh both the world surrounded by the great ocean of expanding waves, and the rarely attained, glorious world of the gods, they would not equal in value a single day for us, spent in embracing the shoulders of this young woman with flower-like kohl-rimmed eyes, golden complexion, and beautiful lines on her loins.",
 "modern": "விரிந்த அலைகளைக் கொண்ட பெரிய கடலால் சூழப்பட்ட இந்த உலகமும், அடைவதற்கு அரிய சிறப்புகளைக் கொண்ட தேவர்களின் உலகமும் ஆகிய இவ்விரண்டையும் சீர்தூக்கிப் பார்த்தாலும், மலர் போன்ற மை தீட்டிய கண்களையும், பொன்னிற மேனியையும், அழகிய வரிகளைக் கொண்ட அல்குலையும் உடைய இளமங்கையின் தோள்களைத் தழுவி மகிழும் ஒரு நாளுக்கு அவை இணையாக மாட்டா.",
 "explanation": "The hero speaks to himself, expressing the incomparable joy of union with his beloved. He compares the entire earth surrounded by the vast ocean and the heavenly world of the gods to a single day spent in the embrace of his beloved. He declares that even if both these worlds were placed on a scale, they would not equal the value of exchanging embraces with her. This poem belongs to the kuriñci (mountainous) landscape, which is themed around union (puṇartal). The intense focus on physical and emotional union and the hyperbolic comparison highlight the supreme value of love over worldly or heavenly riches. Key words include 'tōḷmāṟu paṭūum vaikal' (the days of exchanging embraces) and 'cīr sālā' (not equal in weight).",
